{
  "abstract": "Do the six regular-quintuple equations of a hexagon force its isolated seventh entry to take exceptional values? We prove that their nondegenerate locus has a two-dimensional component dominating the isolated-entry line, as conjectured in the earlier study of this window system. We give an explicit double plane parametrizing the component by two signed ratios. Its parity triangles differ by a translation on an elliptic cubic. After a cyclic isogeny of degree three, one quadratic character controls all nine roots joining the two triangles. A rank-one elliptic section makes those nine roots rational and yields infinitely many septuples whose entries and all 21 roots lie in totally real fields of degree at most four. We determine the rationality of the isolated entry on that section. A homogeneous residue calculation and a two-descent on a genus-two Jacobian show that every ordinary rational parameter gives an irrational isolated entry. Imposing a seventh regular-quintuple equation also yields an explicit degree-26 component. Its genus-two normalization and elliptic quotient exclude positive rational squared anchors on this component, including the singular fibres. The supplement gives both labelled reconstructions and exact computations in the relevant function fields. The general rational septuple problem remains open.",
